WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.260
From genetic instructions to protein: copy, read, fold.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.460
Let’s follow one tiny gene.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.260
Inside the nucleus, DNA stores the instructions.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.360
Its paired strands separate only where one gene needs to be copied.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.093
RNA polymerase binds a promoter, opening a small bubble to expose the template.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:32.808
During transcription, complementary bases build messenger RNA. T pairs with A, A with U, C with G, and G with C. RNA grows five prime to three prime.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.114
At the gene’s end, the RNA is released and the DNA closes.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.300
Processing removes the noncoding intron and joins the exons.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.240
The mature RNA keeps the same coding sequence.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.580
Messenger RNA travels through a nuclear pore into the cytoplasm.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.020
Each three-base group is a codon.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:05.228
Translation begins at AUG. An initiator transfer RNA pairs its UAC anticodon with that start codon, bringing methionine. The ribosome assembles around them.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:10.364
GGC matches CCG, bringing glycine.

00:01:10.664 --> 00:01:14.258
Peptide bond: methionine joins glycine.

00:01:14.558 --> 00:01:17.274
Empty adapter out; ribosome forward.

00:01:17.574 --> 00:01:22.367
Then UUU pairs with AAA, bringing phenylalanine.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:25.782
The growing chain transfers onto phenylalanine.

00:01:26.082 --> 00:01:28.078
The ribosome advances again.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.360
UGA means stop.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.560
A release factor frees the chain.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.320
No amino acid is added.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.580
The ribosomal subunits separate.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:48.461
Our tiny product is just three amino acids. The longer chain here illustrates protein folding: shape helps determine function.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.820
DNA stores the recipe.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.560
Transcription copies it.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.100
Translation reads it.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.180
Folding shapes a functional protein.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:58.720
Copy, read, fold.
